import json, re, statistics
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(__file__).parent
cases = []
rubrics = {}

def add(id, split, category, prompt, constraints, source_ids, features, include, must_not, critical, source_expectations, verification):
    language = 'German' if any('German' in c for c in constraints) else ('Simplified Chinese' if any('Chinese' in c for c in constraints) else 'English')
    cases.append(dict(id=id, split=split, category=category, prompt=prompt.strip(), constraints={'language':language}, source_ids=source_ids, complexity_features=features))
    rubrics[id] = dict(must_include=include, must_not=must_not, critical_errors=critical, source_expectations=source_expectations, verification=verification)

add('B01','development','scientific_mechanism_prerequisites', '''
Synthetic educational case: every organism, compound, experiment, and document below is invented. Explain why adding the activator does not reliably produce light, and what the experiments actually establish about the proposed mechanism. Write for a laboratory apprentice who knows what an enzyme is but keeps describing every measured increase as proof that the complete pathway is working.

[B01-S1: agreed working model, version 4] In the invented organism Neral, enzyme E converts substrate P into intermediate Q. E requires a charged cofactor C. Protein R carries Q across an intact internal membrane. Inside the compartment, enzyme L converts Q into a light-producing product only when local acidity is below a threshold. Activator A increases the amount of E after a delay; it does not directly charge C, repair the membrane, or change L. These statements summarize the laboratory's working model, rather than laws independently verified in this packet. Whole-cell Q assays add together Q from both sides of the membrane and cannot distinguish the two locations.

[B01-S2: controlled cultures] Identical cultures received the same P. At minute twenty, untreated cultures had E abundance 1, whole-cell Q 10, and light 8 in arbitrary units. Cultures receiving A had E abundance 4, Q 38, and light 9. Cultures receiving A plus a cofactor-charging treatment had E abundance 4, Q 75, and light 10. A third intervention, M, was then added to an A-treated culture: E abundance remained 4, whole-cell Q fell from 38 to 17, and light rose from 9 to 31. M is known from a separate assay to increase membrane transport, but that assay did not measure local acidity. All values are means from three cultures; uncertainty estimates are unavailable.

[B01-S3: perturbation notes] Deleting R eliminated light under all tested conditions. Deletion also damaged the membrane in a way that the investigators have not yet separated from transport loss. Providing Q outside the compartment did not restore light to those deleted cultures. Providing Q inside intact compartments isolated from normal cultures produced light when acidity was below the threshold, even without A. The same preparation produced little light above the threshold. The isolation procedure removes several soluble proteins and supplies charged C, so it is not a neutral reproduction of a whole cell.

[B01-S4: meeting disagreement] One researcher says the fall in whole-cell Q after M proves that E became less active. Another says R is the sole bottleneck and acidity can now be ignored. A purchasing memo says the supplier's activator has a ninety percent customer satisfaction score; the memo supplies no biological measurements. A junior colleague proposes a rescue experiment expressing R in the deleted cultures, but has not yet established whether this would also repair their membranes.

Give a coherent causal explanation that separates production, location, consumption, and the final readout. Discuss why some seemingly decisive observations have more than one interpretation. End by describing a small set of measurements or interventions that would distinguish the live explanations, including what outcome would support each. Do not silently turn the working model or the satisfaction score into additional experimental proof.
''', ['Answer in English','Explain causal dependencies and evidence limits'], ['B01-S1','B01-S2','B01-S3','B01-S4'], ['serial prerequisites','aggregate versus compartment readout','confounded knockout','misleading irrelevant source'],
['A increases E but light additionally depends on charged C, Q transport, membrane integrity, and local acidity/L.','Whole-cell Q cannot identify Q localization; high Q may reflect accumulation before a downstream constraint.','The Q decrease and light increase after M are compatible with faster downstream consumption, not proof that E slowed.','M supports a transport-related explanation but acidity effects remain unmeasured.','R deletion is confounded by membrane damage; neither sole bottleneck nor direct transport necessity is isolated.','Direct intracompartartment Q and acidity manipulation support conditional downstream sufficiency in the isolated preparation, with limits on whole-cell generalization.','Propose discriminating compartment-specific Q/acidity measurements and a rescue that separates transport from membrane repair.'],
['Treat the satisfaction memo as mechanistic evidence','Invent significance or certainty from three means'], ['Claim A alone is sufficient for light','Claim R is the proven sole bottleneck','Infer reduced E activity solely from reduced Q'], 'Attribute model assumptions to S1, experimental results to S2/S3, and proposals or claims to S4; paraphrase source labels accurately.', 'Conceptual consistency against supplied model; no external biological facts required.')

add('B02','development','conflicting_studies_evidence_strength', '''
Synthetic educational research packet. A town is considering an invented roof coating, Luma, intended to reduce summer indoor heat. Explain to a residents' committee why the three reports below appear to disagree, what claim each can support, and whether the packet establishes that Luma improves comfort in the town's actual housing. All numbers and institutions are fictional. Your audience can understand percentages but has not studied experimental design.

[B02-S1: randomized pilot protocol and results] Twenty apartment buildings were randomized, ten to Luma and ten to the existing coating. The protocol specified average afternoon indoor temperature over eight weeks as the primary outcome. Buildings contained between twelve and eighty occupied apartments. The estimated difference was minus 0.6 temperature units, with a cluster-adjusted uncertainty interval from minus 1.4 to plus 0.2. A later analyst treated all 740 apartments as independent and obtained a narrower interval excluding zero. Allocation occurred at building level; apartments shared ventilation and roof exposure. Occupants knew which coating their building received. The preregistered comfort questionnaire had a 78 percent response rate in controls and a 51 percent response rate in treated buildings, and the report does not give reasons for nonresponse.

[B02-S2: contractor's comparison] A contractor compared 120 voluntarily coated buildings with 120 uncoated buildings. Coated buildings reported 30 percent fewer afternoons of discomfort. Their owners also installed external shades in 70 percent of cases, compared with 15 percent of uncoated buildings. The contractor adjusted for building age but not shades, insulation, neighborhood, or previous discomfort. Owners elected whether to enter the survey after hearing their own residents' complaints. The report calls the larger sample definitive and describes the randomized pilot as underpowered. No raw comfort scores or recruitment list are available.

[B02-S3: laboratory chamber report] In six paired roof panels exposed to fixed radiation, Luma reduced inward heat flow by 18 percent. The chamber maintained equal airflow, dry surfaces, and no internal heat sources. The tested substrate matches only one of the town's three common roof materials. In another experiment with a wet surface, the estimated reduction was 3 percent, but the report gives neither replicate count nor uncertainty. These are physical measurements, not resident assessments. The manufacturer funded the report, and the methods and full numerical panel results are available; funding alone neither validates nor invalidates the measurements.

[B02-S4: implementation context] Most local discomfort complaints arise in top-floor apartments during humid evenings. The pilot's endpoint was afternoon temperature, and half its buildings were outside the town in a drier area. A press release says Luma has won an architecture design award. A committee member concludes that an interval crossing zero proves no effect. Another concludes that the chamber proves the contractor's comfort estimate is causal. A third asks whether a smaller average benefit could still matter for the hottest apartments.

Build the explanation around what was assigned or selected, what was measured, and where each result might transfer. Give a provisional conclusion without converting uncertainty into either certainty of benefit or certainty of no effect. Identify the most valuable next study or missing information for the committee's question, and explain why it would resolve a specific gap rather than merely add more observations.
''', ['Answer in English','Use only the synthetic packet for factual claims'], ['B02-S1','B02-S2','B02-S3','B02-S4'], ['cluster randomization','selection and confounding','endpoint mismatch','external validity','misleading award'],
['Randomized assignment is at building level, making the cluster-adjusted interval appropriate to the described design.','The pilot estimate is compatible with a range including modest benefit and a small worsening; crossing zero does not prove zero effect.','Voluntary coating, differential shading, and complaint-driven selection prevent a clean causal comfort interpretation.','The chamber supports a physical mechanism under controlled conditions but does not establish real-world comfort magnitude.','Differential questionnaire response threatens observed comfort comparisons even in a randomized trial.','Afternoon versus humid-evening conditions, substrate, and town composition limit transfer.','Recommend a locally relevant randomized building-level study or targeted missing data tied to comfort, nonresponse, extreme apartments, and humidity.'],
['Treat the design award as effectiveness evidence','Reject a study solely because it is industry funded'], ['Count all apartments as independently randomized','Claim either zero effect or established 30 percent causal comfort reduction'], 'Discuss S1-S3 separately before synthesis; use S4 to define the target question, not as outcome evidence.', 'No calculation required beyond interpreting supplied estimates; study-design claims checked against assignment and endpoint descriptions.')

add('B03','development','fictional_policy_scope_dates', '''
This is a wholly synthetic administrative exercise, not advice about real law. Explain how the invented Port Meridian equipment rules apply to the three shipments below. The logistics team has contradictory documents and keeps applying whichever date appears latest on a page. Give a shipment-by-shipment explanation and identify any decision that depends on a fact the packet does not establish.

[B03-S1: enacted ordinance, published April 2] Starting June 1, commercial equipment first placed into service within Port Meridian requires an inspection certificate. Equipment physically delivered to a customer before June 1 may use a transition declaration instead, provided its signed purchase agreement predates April 2. The delivery exception concerns physical delivery, not dispatch, customs clearance, or invoice creation. The ordinance covers the port district only; the neighboring Estuary district has its own regime, which is not included here. Private household equipment is outside this ordinance. An implementing office may specify forms but may not alter eligibility dates.

[B03-S2: implementing circular, published May 10] Transition declarations must include a signed agreement, proof of physical customer delivery, and the equipment serial number. Declarations may be filed until July 15. Filing after first use is allowed if the substantive transition conditions were met; filing does not itself make otherwise ineligible equipment eligible. The office's May 11 training slide summarizes this as “orders received before April qualify through July.” The slide omits the delivery condition and uses “orders received” without defining whether that means an email inquiry or a contract. Its footer says it is an informal training aid.

[B03-S3: proposed amendment, posted May 28] A draft would move the physical-delivery cutoff to June 15 for equipment delayed by dock repairs. Its cover page says “consultation closes June 12; enactment requires a separate council vote.” A news digest dated June 3 calls this the “June extension” but links only to the draft. Nothing in this packet records a vote, promulgation, or an amended ordinance. A sales brochure praises the port's rapid modernization and includes photographs of renovated cranes.

[B03-S4: shipment facts] Unit Alder has a signed commercial purchase agreement dated March 29, reached the port warehouse May 29, was physically delivered to its Port Meridian customer June 3, and first operated June 5. Unit Birch has a signed agreement dated April 1, was physically delivered May 31 to its Port Meridian commercial customer, first operated June 2, and has a declaration filing appointment July 10. Unit Cedar has a signed agreement dated March 20, was delivered May 25 to a warehouse whose postal address says Meridian Bay, and first operated June 4 in a leased workshop. The map defining the port district and the workshop's district assignment are missing. All three units are used commercially, and none is used in a private home.

The manager wants one explanation they can use to correct the team's decision process. Distinguish publication, agreement, delivery, service, and filing dates rather than calling every date a deadline. Explain the document hierarchy in this fictional scheme. Where a conditional answer is necessary, say precisely which fact is missing and how either possible answer would change the relevant conclusion. Do not assume the proposed extension became effective because a later article used confident language.
''', ['Answer in English','Label conclusions as interpretation of fictional supplied rules'], ['B03-S1','B03-S2','B03-S3','B03-S4'], ['source authority','effective versus procedural dates','jurisdiction ambiguity','draft versus enacted text'],
['June 1 triggers the certificate requirement for covered equipment first put into service then or later.','Transition eligibility requires both an agreement before April 2 and physical customer delivery before June 1.','Alder fails the enacted delivery condition despite warehouse arrival, so requires a certificate under the supplied current rule.','Birch satisfies both transition conditions; July 10 filing is within July 15 and post-service filing is permitted.','Cedar cannot be resolved without the workshop jurisdiction; postal wording and warehouse location do not prove covered service location.','Distinguish binding ordinance/circular from informal slide and unenacted proposal/news digest.','State that the packet does not establish enactment of the proposed extension and that outside-port rules are absent.'],
['Use renovation publicity as eligibility evidence','Equate warehouse delivery with customer delivery'], ['Apply the proposed June 15 cutoff as enacted','Declare Cedar definitively covered or exempt without location evidence','Deny Birch solely because filing follows use'], 'Pin substantive dates to S1, filing rules to S2, draft status to S3, and shipment facts/unknowns to S4.', 'Date comparisons independently checked; no real jurisdictional research appropriate.')

add('B04','development','operations_censored_demand', '''
Synthetic business analysis exercise. A fictional distributor trialed a forecasting system in two stores and announced that it reduced waste while preserving service. Explain to the operations director what can and cannot be learned from the evidence. The director wants a causal story, not just a list of metrics, and is deciding whether to expand the trial to stores with different customer patterns.

[B04-S1: inventory ledger] During the four baseline weeks, North received 1,000 items, sold 900, and discarded 100; South received 400, sold 360, and discarded 40. During the four trial weeks, North received 800, sold 760, and discarded 40; South received 600, sold 570, and discarded 30. Opening and closing stock were zero in both periods, and transfers between stores were prohibited. Discarded items had expired. Selling price was 10 currency units and acquisition cost was 6 per item; disposal cost and other expenses were unchanged but their amounts are not provided. The financial team calls sales divided by receipts the “service rate.”

[B04-S2: availability logs] North recorded 2 hours without stock during baseline and 18 during trial. South recorded 1 hour without stock during baseline and 1 during trial. Each store was open 200 hours in each period. The counter records only completed purchases. It does not count customers who left without buying, substituted another item, or never entered after seeing an empty display. In two trial stockouts, staff wrote that queues were unusually long, but no systematic queue count exists. The system's marketing page says customers value predictability; it contains no data from these stores.

[B04-S3: rollout design] Both stores adopted the system at the start of the trial period. North simultaneously lost a nearby bus stop for road construction. South began supplying a newly opened office cafeteria, which purchased 120 items in the trial period. No comparison stores were retained. Temperature and local events also changed, but their relationship with demand is unknown. The distributor had selected these stores because baseline waste was unusually high. The forecasting system's training data contained historical sales and delivery quantities, not attempted purchases or stockout-adjusted demand.

[B04-S4: internal claims] The trial report says waste fell from 140 to 70 items, so demand forecasting must have improved by fifty percent. It also says the aggregate service rate increased from ninety percent to ninety-five percent, proving fewer customers were disappointed. A store manager argues that the increase in North's stockout hours proves total customer demand rose. A finance analyst proposes expanding immediately because aggregate sales revenue increased; the analyst has not subtracted item acquisition costs or considered the possibility that South's new contract would have increased sales without the system.

Explain the arithmetic and the competing causal interpretations in a way that keeps observed sales separate from latent demand and service quality. Calculate the change in contribution before unchanged expenses, using the supplied accounting assumptions. Suggest a feasible evaluation that could distinguish improved matching from simply ordering too little or shifting stock toward a new customer. State the exact evidence limit on estimating unmet demand from these records, and explain why even a successful reconstruction of demand would not by itself establish the system's causal effect.
''', ['Answer in English','Show key arithmetic and units'], ['B04-S1','B04-S2','B04-S3','B04-S4'], ['censored demand','before-after confounding','metric naming trap','contribution accounting','regression to mean'],
['Receipts total 1400 in both periods; sales rise from 1260 to 1330 and expired waste falls 140 to 70.','Sales/receipts rises 90% to 95% but measures sell-through, not probability an attempted customer purchase succeeds.','Observed stockout hours increase at North and do not identify unmet demand or demand direction because supply also changes.','Contribution before unchanged expenses rises from 4200 to 4900, a 700-unit increase.','Road construction, cafeteria contract, season/time changes, and selection on high waste block a clean causal attribution.','Sales are censored by inventory availability; exact unmet demand is unidentifiable without attempted demand or justified external assumptions.','Propose comparison or randomized phased rollout with lost-demand/availability capture and separate waste, service, and profit outcomes.'],
['Assume every stockout hour has the same lost demand','Interpret marketing prose as trial evidence'], ['Claim 50% better forecast accuracy from halved waste','Call 95% a measured customer service success probability','Calculate profit by charging acquisition cost only to sold items'], 'Use S1 for arithmetic, S2 for observability, S3 for identification, and treat S4 as claims to assess.', 'Execute receipt/sales/waste/contribution arithmetic in verification script.')

add('B05','development','historical_archive_inference', '''
The following is an original synthetic archive from the invented river town of Leth. No real people or historical events are involved. Explain what the documents support about whether the town's “night names” were chiefly a tool of resistance, a trading convenience, or an administrative category. A museum curator wants a label that can present disagreement without pretending the evidence is evenly strong for every claim.

[B05-S1: harbor register, year 41] The surviving register lists 62 traders under daytime household names. Beside 19 names, a different hand later added a short night name. Thirteen of those 19 traders operated boats after dusk. Six did not have boat occupations recorded. The register's first and last pages are missing. A marginal note says, “Use the short name when the tide turns; the household answers to the tax.” The date of the annotation is unknown, and an archivist has not established whether the marginal note and the short names were written by the same clerk. The register records taxable movements, not private naming practices.

[B05-S2: merchants' letter, year 44] Four traders jointly wrote: “The inspectors ask for the house, the boatmen answer with the water name, and an hour passes before the barley moves. Let the clerk keep both.” Their petition requests faster clearance and makes no explicit claim about political freedom. The only surviving copy was entered into an official petition book by an unknown clerk. Names in the signatory list match three annotated register entries; the fourth has no surviving register entry. A historian proposes that an official copy could soften confrontational language, but no independent version exists.

[B05-S3: memoir written in year 79] A former watch officer recalls events in year 43: “We could arrest a household, but never catch its night name. The young laughed at our ledgers.” In another chapter he admits confusing two flood seasons in an earlier telling. His memoir was sold during a campaign against a new tax authority. It includes accurate descriptions of three gates confirmed by surviving architectural plans. Those confirmations concern geography; they do not independently verify his account of naming practices or the motives of traders.

[B05-S4: song fragment, copy dated year 88] “My mother gave a name to bread; the river gave a name to hunger. At the gate I carried one, at home I buried neither.” The fragment is unsigned. Its original composition date is unknown, and the collector grouped it with “old resistance songs.” A catalog added in year 112 paraphrases it as proof that citizens abandoned household identities. No text in the fragment says that one name replaced the other. The same collector also grouped unrelated wedding verses under the resistance heading.

The museum has room for a reasoned explanation, not a definitive verdict about all residents. Discuss the different kinds of evidence, the difference between a practice's function and participants' intentions, and how later political uses can coexist with earlier practical uses. Offer the strongest defensible synthesis and one alternative that remains live. State what cannot be inferred about prevalence, chronology, or motive from this selected archive. Do not fill gaps with invented customs, and do not treat a later catalog heading as if it were contemporary testimony.
''', ['Answer in English','Interpret only original synthetic texts'], ['B05-S1','B05-S2','B05-S3','B05-S4'], ['source provenance','retrospective motive','selection in archive','function versus intent','competing interpretation'],
['Register associates some night names with nocturnal boating and tax administration but does not establish universal practice or annotation timing.','The merchants’ petition directly supports a clearance/coordination function and dual-name administration.','Resistance is supported by retrospective testimony, with temporal, political, and memory limitations rather than automatic dismissal.','Accurate gate descriptions do not validate independent claims about trader motives.','Song language allows dual identities and does not say household names were abandoned.','Different practical, administrative, and resistant functions can coexist or change over time.','Give a weighted synthesis plus a plausible live alternative and explicit limits on prevalence, chronology, and motives.'],
['Invent external traditions or historical facts','Treat all sources as equally reliable about all questions'], ['Assert the catalog proves original resistance intent','Claim the register establishes town-wide prevalence','Claim night names replaced household identities'], 'Cite source labels or precise supplied passages; distinguish contemporary records, retrospective memoir, uncertain-date song, and later catalog.', 'Qualitative source criticism cross-check; no external historical facts used.')

add('B06','development','chinese_security_api_evidence', r'''
Synthetic software documentation exercise. All services, secrets, organizations, and endpoints are invented. Please explain the incident below in simplified Chinese to an engineer who can read code but is new to the distinction between authentication, authorization, and replay prevention. Keep endpoint paths, header names, identifiers, and code tokens exactly as written. The explanation should answer the team's actual disagreement using this packet; do not substitute general claims about what well-designed APIs usually do.

[B06-S1: current gateway contract, version 3] The gateway authenticates a caller using HMAC_SHA256(secret, method + "\n" + path + "\n" + timestamp + "\n" + SHA256(body_bytes)). The timestamp must be within 60 seconds of gateway time. A recognized key identifies a client account. The gateway forwards authenticated requests to the service, adding X-Client-ID. This version does not include a nonce store and does not promise at-most-once execution. A key may have an optional list of allowed methods, but all keys in this incident permit POST. The route path contains a tenant identifier. The contract states that services must independently enforce tenant membership and resource permissions.

[B06-S2: order service code]
    client = request.header("X-Client-ID")
    tenant = request.path_param("tenant")
    if not directory.is_member(client, tenant):
        return 403
    order = parse_json(request.body)
    save_order(tenant, order)
    return 201
The service is reachable only through the gateway in the described deployment. The directory query is accurate for the supplied incident times. There is no uniqueness check on order.reference, no idempotency-key handling, and no transaction identifier shared between repeated submissions. The packet does not say whether save_order has any undocumented deduplication behavior, so do not invent one.

[B06-S3: incident timeline] At 10:00:00, client K, a member of tenant T1 but not tenant T2, signed POST /tenants/T1/orders with body {"reference":"R7","amount":40}. At 10:00:08, the gateway accepted it and the service returned 201. The client's response connection then failed. At 10:00:15, a retry sent identical method, path, timestamp, body, and signature. It also received 201. Storage logs show two different order identifiers with reference R7 in T1. At 10:00:20, someone changed the captured path to /tenants/T2/orders without recomputing the signature. The gateway rejected this request before forwarding. At 10:00:25, K's client software deliberately computed a valid signature over the T2 path using K's legitimate key; the gateway accepted it and the service returned 403.

[B06-S4: disputed documents] An archived version 1 overview says “signed requests can never be repeated,” but describes a nonce database that version 3 removed. A slide says “a valid signature grants access” without mentioning services. An unrelated reliability report says the gateway has 99.99 percent availability. One engineer concludes that the duplicate orders prove HMAC is broken. Another says that signing a tenant path automatically grants membership in that tenant. A third wants to reject every repeated signature globally, without considering legitimate retries or retention windows.

Explain what each observed response demonstrates and what it does not. Recommend a coherent place to enforce tenant authorization and a retry design that can give this operation stable results without pretending timestamps are unique request identifiers. Distinguish the documented current behavior from proposed changes, and include the precise limits of the incident evidence.
''', ['Answer in simplified Chinese','Preserve exact code/API tokens','Source-bound security explanation'], ['B06-S1','B06-S2','B06-S3','B06-S4'], ['multilingual output','authentication authorization replay distinction','version conflict','incident trace','misleading reliability metric'],
['Explain in Chinese that a valid HMAC authenticates client/account and integrity of signed fields, not tenant membership or one-time use.','The timestamp limits accepted age but permits repeated identical requests within 60 seconds.','Both T1 submissions passed and created distinct orders because current operation lacks documented idempotency; observed logs establish duplication for this incident.','Changing T1 to T2 without re-signing fails integrity verification; re-signing with legitimate K key passes authentication but fails membership authorization.','Tenant authorization belongs in the service or equally authoritative enforced layer, consistent with the current contract.','Recommend a scoped idempotency key tied to payload/operation, atomic record-and-result handling, and defined retry/retention/conflict semantics.','Prefer current S1 over obsolete S4 overview and distinguish proposed protections from observed guarantees.'],
['Translate or alter endpoint paths and header names','Treat availability as replay protection','Claim HMAC encryption hides the body'], ['Claim duplicates demonstrate cryptographic break','Claim a signed tenant path grants membership','Claim a timestamp guarantees uniqueness','Ignore Chinese language requirement'], 'Use S1/S2 to explain the four outcomes in S3; explicitly resolve S4 version and shorthand conflicts.', 'Trace branches against supplied pseudocode and timestamp window; no cryptographic attack or external API facts required.')

add('B07','development','german_energy_accounting', '''
Synthetic educational engineering packet. The invented HeatNest apparatus stores energy and later heats a demonstration room. A visitor has been told that its output is four times its input and therefore it “makes energy.” Explain the measurements and the disagreement in German for a technically curious visitor. Preserve the units kW and kWh and distinguish power from energy. You may use the supplied balance assumptions, but do not import undocumented features such as heat pumps or chemical fuel.

[B07-S1: stated measurement boundary] The apparatus contains an electrical converter and a storage reservoir. The room receives useful heat through one measured outlet. Other heat leaving the apparatus goes to the corridor and is counted as a loss for this demonstration. There are no fuel, material, solar, or environmental heat inputs during either measured phase. A calibration translates reservoir state into stored energy; the stated changes below already use that calibration. The demonstration excludes a monitoring computer that consumes 3 kWh over the complete experiment. The computer does not supply heat to the room or reservoir. The report must state whether it is discussing the apparatus boundary or the larger installation boundary.

[B07-S2: discharge phase] The apparatus runs for 10 hours. Average electrical input is 2 kW, and average useful heat flow to the room is 8 kW. Stored reservoir energy falls by 70 kWh. The heat-loss meter records 10 kWh to the corridor. At the start, the reservoir had been charged previously; the electricity used for that earlier charge is not part of the discharge meter reading. A salesperson divides 8 by 2, obtains four, and labels the result “four hundred percent whole-system efficiency.” Another observer notices that the reservoir temperature falls and concludes that all measured room heat came from the reservoir, ignoring the electrical input.

[B07-S3: recharge phase] Immediately after discharge, the apparatus recharges for 5 hours at an average electrical input of 18 kW. It delivers no useful heat to the room in this phase. The reservoir gains 70 kWh, returning to its initial state, and 20 kWh escapes to the corridor. All meters cover their entire respective phases. There are no missing operating intervals between the end of discharge and the end of recharge. The operator calls recharge a “maintenance event” and leaves it out of the headline performance figure, although the next identical discharge cannot begin without restoring the reservoir.

[B07-S4: disputed interpretation] A brochure describes an unrelated award for compact industrial design. A critic argues that the four-to-one ratio must be a faulty meter reading because conservation forbids any useful output larger than simultaneous electrical input. A second critic calculates a full-cycle ratio but adds 2 kW directly to 90 kWh. A third says including corridor losses as useful output would be dishonest under any conceivable purpose, even if a different customer actually wanted to heat that corridor.

Explain which ratios are arithmetically correct, what each measures, and why the discharge ratio does not establish energy creation. Reconcile both phases with the supplied energy balance and calculate full-cycle useful efficiency at the apparatus boundary. Briefly show what changes when the monitoring computer is included. Discuss why the meaning of “useful” depends on the stated purpose, while the physical balance does not. Do not claim that these measurements establish performance in a different operating environment.
''', ['Answer in German','Preserve kW/kWh distinction','Use stated physical boundary'], ['B07-S1','B07-S2','B07-S3','B07-S4'], ['multilingual output','storage versus input','cycle boundary','power versus energy','normative useful-output definition'],
['German explanation distinguishes instantaneous/average power in kW from time-integrated energy in kWh.','Discharge electrical input is 20 kWh and useful output 80 kWh; ratio four is correct for that phase’s electricity-only denominator.','Discharge balance is 20 + 70 = 80 + 10 kWh, so storage withdrawal accounts for output above electrical input.','Recharge uses 90 kWh: 70 replenishes storage and 20 is lost.','Whole apparatus cycle uses 110 kWh, produces 80 useful kWh, loses 30 kWh, and has zero net storage change; useful efficiency is about 72.7%.','Including 3 kWh computer consumption yields 80/113, about 70.8%, for the larger stated boundary.','The label useful is purpose-dependent; excluding necessary recharge or mixing kW with kWh misstates the requested cycle measure.'],
['Invent environmental heat extraction','Use design award as energetic evidence'], ['Claim energy creation or a conservation violation despite closed balance','Call four-to-one the full-cycle efficiency','Ignore German requirement'], 'Tie boundaries to S1 and phase calculations to S2/S3; treat S4 as claims requiring assessment.', 'Execute both phase balances, cycle totals, and efficiency ratios.')

add('B08','development','queueing_metrics_nonstationary', '''
All services and numbers in this operational incident are synthetic. Explain why an API dashboard can report a low average latency while a large backlog remains, and whether the supplied metrics justify doubling the worker count. The audience is an operations manager who has heard that average work in a system equals arrival rate times average time in the system but does not know the assumptions behind applying that relationship to a short incident window.

[B08-S1: ten-minute window] At the beginning of the window, 120 admitted jobs were unfinished. During the next 600 seconds, 600 new jobs were admitted, 540 jobs completed, and no jobs were canceled, dropped, or transferred. At the end, 180 jobs were unfinished. A continuously sampled gauge reported a time-weighted mean of 150 unfinished jobs, including both waiting and executing jobs. The gauge counts jobs awaiting an external dependency as unfinished. The arrival counter counts admission into this same system, not attempted requests rejected before admission. There were no counter resets during the window.

[B08-S2: latency histogram] The mean end-to-end time among jobs that completed during the window was 20 seconds, and their ninety-fifth percentile was 25 seconds. Each duration starts at that job's original admission, including any time before the window. The histogram contains only completed jobs. It excludes the 180 still unfinished at the end. Several initially unfinished jobs had already waited for more than an hour before the window began. An engineer multiplies one admitted job per second by twenty seconds and concludes that the gauge must be wrong because it should read twenty rather than one hundred fifty.

[B08-S3: worker and dependency observations] Forty workers were configured, but average CPU utilization was 22 percent. Workers can release their execution slot while an external request remains pending, so the unfinished-job gauge is not a worker-occupancy gauge. During the incident, a shared dependency intermittently stopped responding to one class of jobs, while another class continued normally. The logs do not identify the classes of all unfinished jobs. The dependency team has not yet supplied its latency or rate-limit data. A sample of completed jobs therefore need not describe the jobs that remain stuck.

[B08-S4: discussion] One analyst calls 540 divided by 600 the arrival rate, although that number counts completions per second. Another says the ninety-fifth percentile proves that at least ninety-five percent of all admitted jobs finished within twenty-five seconds. A third estimates that the backlog will clear in exactly 180 divided by 0.9 seconds after admissions stop, assuming the measured completion rate continues. A status page says the service met its quarterly uptime target; that page neither measures this window's queue behavior nor defines job completion time.

Give a careful explanation of the flow balance, the denominators in the latency statistics, and the role of finite-window boundary jobs. Explain why the discrepancy does not by itself falsify either instrument, without asserting that every possible dataset with these summaries is equally plausible. State what can be calculated directly and what requires a stability or representativeness assumption. Recommend the few additional measurements most useful for deciding between worker shortage, dependency blocking, and workload mix. The manager needs to understand the causal mechanism before accepting a scaling proposal.
''', ['Answer in English','Explain assumptions behind queue identity use'], ['B08-S1','B08-S2','B08-S3','B08-S4'], ['finite window accounting','completion selection','nonstationarity','percentile denominator','capacity decision uncertainty'],
['Flow balance is 120 + 600 - 540 = 180; arrivals average 1/s and completions 0.9/s.','The system grows by 60 unfinished jobs in the window, so steady-state use needs justification.','Completed-job mean/percentiles condition on completion and exclude stuck jobs; the admitted cohort claim is unsupported.','Boundary jobs and differing time/observation populations explain why L=150 cannot be combined mechanically with completed-only W=20.','The gauge implies 90000 job-seconds within the window, while completed durations alone do not represent all that occupancy.','A 200-second clearance estimate assumes sustained 0.9/s with no new admissions and comparable remaining work; it is not established.','CPU and released slots make blocked dependencies plausible; obtain age/class/state distributions, worker occupancy and dependency traces before assuming more workers fix the issue.'],
['Treat quarterly uptime as queue evidence','Assert exactly which unfinished jobs are dependency-blocked'], ['Claim the metrics are mathematically impossible solely because 150 differs from 20','Claim p95 completed latency bounds all admissions','Recommend doubling workers as a proven fix'], 'Anchor rates and balance to S1, selection to S2, and mechanism uncertainty to S3; critique S4 explicitly.', 'Execute flow balance, job-seconds, rates, and conditional clearance arithmetic.')

add('B09','development','literary_form_unreliable_evidence', '''
This is an original synthetic literary passage and editorial packet. Explain how the passage makes the reader uncertain about Mara's responsibility for the opening of the floodgate. The reader wants a close explanation of the writing, not a plot summary or an invented biography of an author. You may argue for a leading interpretation, but show what in the text resists it and distinguish the narrator's confidence from evidence that the event actually occurred as described.

[B09-S1: passage, first movement] I remember the gate opening because I remember being innocent. The bell had not yet sounded, unless what I heard was the bell before the bell, the small iron cough they used to test the rope. Mara stood beside the wheel with both hands hidden in her sleeves. “You have the key,” I told her. She looked at my empty belt and said, “Then why do you keep reaching for it?” Beyond her, the water lay flat as a letter no one had opened. Our brother's boat was not visible. I called that proof that he had escaped, although in those days I called many absences proof.

[B09-S2: passage, second movement] The keeper arrived carrying a lantern in daylight. He put it on the ground between us, where it made no difference to what we could see. “Who asked for the gate?” he said. I answered, “She did,” at the same instant Mara said, “We did.” The keeper did not write either answer. He asked instead whether the lower houses had been warned. Mara took one hand from her sleeve. Her palm was red, but the wheel was red too, and the town had painted every public handle for the festival. I wanted the color to be an accusation. It was easier to look at her hand than at the path toward the houses.

[B09-S3: passage, final movement] Years later, when I had become the keeper, I changed the notice above the wheel. The old notice said, “No one opens this gate alone.” My new notice said, “Two witnesses required.” People praised the clarity. I kept the old board under my bed, with its face toward the floor, and sometimes imagined it speaking in Mara's voice. The records say the gate opened at noon. My memory says it opened when she stopped answering me. Neither tells me when we first heard the shouting from below.

[B09-S4: editorial materials] A draft margin reads, “Keep the lantern unnecessary.” An early discarded ending said, “Mara turned the wheel,” but the final text omits that sentence. A fictional publisher's jacket describes the work as “the confession of a coward”; the jacket was written after publication by a marketing editor. No external narrative confirms who physically opened the gate, whether the brother survived, or whether warning the houses was possible in time. A classroom handout says a red palm is a universal literary code for guilt, without showing evidence from this text.

Explain how the shifts in time, repeated claims of proof, objects, and competing forms of record shape the problem of responsibility. Pay attention to the difference between individual action, shared authorization, and failure to warn. Discuss the two versions of the gate notice and why apparently clearer wording might narrow an older moral demand. Use the draft and jacket only with their proper evidential status. The final explanation should give a reader ways to understand the ambiguity while preserving the specific unanswered questions that create it.
''', ['Answer in English','Use textual evidence','Preserve unresolved event facts'], ['B09-S1','B09-S2','B09-S3','B09-S4'], ['unreliable narration','temporal reconstruction','multiple responsibility concepts','paratext authority','ambiguous symbols'],
['Narrator’s opening innocence claim and self-correction about absences undermine straightforward evidentiary reliability.','Red hand is ambiguous because painted handles supply another explanation; guilt cannot be decoded universally.','“She did” versus “We did” distinguishes individual blame from shared authorization without settling physical operation.','Keeper’s warning question and narrator’s avoided path shift attention toward omitted collective responsibility.','The unnecessary lantern, hidden hands/key, and written records can support interpretations if tied to specific textual functions rather than fixed universal codes.','Notice change can narrow a communal prohibition/demand into procedural witnesses, while retaining other defensible readings.','Final-text uncertainty outranks discarded explicit ending; jacket and draft are interpretive context, not proof of final events.','State that opener, brother’s fate, and timing/possibility of warnings remain unestablished; support a leading reading and counterevidence.'],
['Invent author biography','Treat every symbol as a fixed code'], ['State Mara certainly opened the gate based on discarded ending','Claim red palm proves guilt','Present jacket interpretation as established narrator confession'], 'Quote or closely reference S1-S3; distinguish final text from S4 draft, marketing, and handout.', 'Qualitative cross-check that claims do not resolve facts intentionally left open.')

add('B10','development','metrics_composition_causal_fairness', '''
Synthetic service-design case. A repair network has introduced a tool that recommends which incoming requests should be accepted for a seven-day repair promise. Explain an apparent disagreement between its overall success rate and its success rates within two difficulty groups. The network also wants to know whether the tool improved customer outcomes and treated two districts fairly. No real people, demographic groups, or companies are represented.

[B10-S1: accepted-request results] In the baseline period, 100 accepted requests were classified Easy before acceptance, and 90 were completed within seven days. Another 100 were classified Hard before acceptance, and 20 were completed within seven days. In the tool period, 20 accepted requests were Easy, of which 19 completed within seven days, while 200 accepted requests were Hard, of which 60 completed within seven days. The Easy/Hard definition was fixed before the trial and did not change. All accepted requests have complete seven-day outcomes. A request that finishes later is a failure for this metric even if the customer eventually receives a satisfactory repair.

[B10-S2: selection and context] The tool was installed throughout the network on the first day of the new period. At the same time, a new contract required accepting many difficult repairs. Managers could override tool recommendations but did not record their reasons. The number and outcomes of rejected or abandoned requests were not retained. Technicians also received new training. There was no randomized allocation, concurrent untreated site, or stable acceptance threshold. The network cannot assume that an accepted Hard request in the two periods has the same distribution of unrecorded severity, despite sharing a category label.

[B10-S3: competing presentations] One presentation reports success within Easy requests rising from 90 percent to 95 percent, and within Hard requests rising from 20 percent to 30 percent. It concludes that the overall rate must have improved and that any contrary figure is an arithmetic error. Another presentation pools all accepted requests, observes a decline, and concludes that the tool harmed every type of customer. A third reweights the tool-period category rates to the baseline's fifty-fifty category mix and calls that value the “causal benefit of the tool.” It does not discuss changed training, selection within categories, or the contract.

[B10-S4: districts and objectives] Incoming requests came from districts East and West. The database stores district but the supplied packet gives no cross-tabulation of district, category, acceptance, or outcome. Network policy says comparable requests should receive comparable access to the promise; it does not define the relevant comparison variables or a numerical fairness criterion. A communications memo calls equal success rates among accepted requests sufficient proof of fairness, without considering access. A customer council also cares about waiting time before acceptance, total successful repairs, and how many people are turned away. None of these outcomes is fully captured by the reported proportion.

Explain the arithmetic with an intuitive account of composition, then separate descriptive standardization from causal identification. Calculate the overall rates and the reweighted tool-period rate using the baseline category mix. Discuss what these quantities answer and what they leave out. End with a defensible evaluation proposal that measures access as well as completion and gives the districts an explicit comparison framework. Do not infer district-specific results or unmet demand from a table that contains neither, and do not use the unchanged category labels as proof that all confounding has been removed.
''', ['Answer in English','Show arithmetic','State missing fairness evidence'], ['B10-S1','B10-S2','B10-S3','B10-S4'], ['Simpson composition reversal','standardization versus causal adjustment','post-selection outcomes','fairness target ambiguity','missing cross-tab'],
['Baseline pooled rate is 110/200=55%; tool period is 79/220≈35.9%.','Within-category rates rise from 90% to 95% and 20% to 30%, while Hard share rises from 50% to about 90.9%.','Composition explains coexistence of conditional improvements and pooled decline; neither claim in S3 is a necessary arithmetic implication.','Baseline-mix standardized tool rate is 0.5×95%+0.5×30%=62.5%, versus baseline 55%.','The 7.5 percentage-point standardized difference is descriptive, not identified causal effect, given training, contract, overrides, and within-category selection.','Accepted-only outcomes omit rejected/abandoned requests and cannot establish access, total customer benefit, or district fairness.','Propose recorded incoming cohorts and district/category acceptance-outcome data, an explicit fairness objective, and controlled/randomized evaluation where feasible.'],
['Invent East/West differences','Equate late completion with no eventual customer benefit'], ['Claim improved subgroup rates force improved overall rate','Claim a pooled decline proves harm in every subgroup','Label standardized difference established causal benefit'], 'Calculate from S1, assess causal limits using S2, and tie fairness limits to S4’s absent cross-tab and ambiguous policy.', 'Execute pooled, within-category, composition, and standardized rates.')

add('B11','development','distributed_state_document_conflict', '''
Synthetic API and synchronization exercise. An invented notes service lets a mobile app display edits while disconnected. A customer reported that a deleted note “came back,” and engineers disagree about whether the server's deletion rule failed. Explain the incident using the current contract, code, interface specification, and observed trace. Keep a clear distinction between a user's visible experience and a claim about the canonical server record.

[B11-S1: current server contract, version 2] Each note has a server-owned epoch and a revision within that epoch. A successful deletion atomically increments the epoch, writes a tombstone, and removes the note's current content. Updates must supply the current epoch; an update from an earlier epoch returns 409. Offline clients cannot mint epochs. Recreating a deleted note requires a separate explicit restore operation, which is not used in this incident. The revision field orders accepted updates within one epoch; a high revision from an old epoch does not outrank a newer epoch. The contract describes the primary notes service, not every administrative import tool.

[B11-S2: relevant update handler]
    with atomic_note_lock(note_id):
        stored = load(note_id)
        if request.epoch != stored.epoch:
            return 409
        if stored.is_tombstone:
            return 410
        write_content(note_id, request.content, stored.epoch,
                      stored.revision + 1)
        return 200
The provided deployment uses this handler for mobile updates. The lock covers both reads and writes and is shared with deletion. A design sketch from two years earlier instead proposed comparing wall-clock modification timestamps. The sketch was never labeled an implemented contract, but appears first in an internal search result. A teammate has cited it as proof that a newer phone timestamp overrides deletion.

[B11-S3: interface specification] While offline, the app may render the user's local draft even after another client has changed or deleted the server note. It labels that view “pending sync.” A 409 response preserves the draft in a conflict tray so the user can copy their work. Conflict-tray items are excluded from the ordinary synchronized note list, but the current CSV exporter includes both ordinary and conflict-tray items and provides no status column. The exporter reads local app state, not the primary database. The product team is considering changing this behavior but has not shipped a revision.

[B11-S4: incident evidence] At 09:00, the phone last synchronized note N with epoch 4, revision 8. At 09:10, a browser deleted N; the server recorded a tombstone at epoch 5. The offline phone later edited its local copy, labeling its draft epoch 4 and local revision 9. At 09:30, it displayed that draft with “pending sync.” At 09:40, its update request received 409. At 09:41, a CSV export contained the draft's title. A direct server GET at 09:42 returned 404 with tombstone epoch 5. The packet includes no trace of administrative imports, backups, or other notes.

Explain why the word “resurrection” can describe more than one claim here. Walk through which branch handles the mobile update and why the CSV is insufficient evidence of canonical restoration. Assess what the evidence establishes for this incident and what additional review would be required to claim deletion can never be bypassed anywhere in the system. Suggest a product clarification that addresses the visible confusion without treating the rejected local draft as if it had been committed.
''', ['Answer in English','Distinguish observed behavior from universal invariant'], ['B11-S1','B11-S2','B11-S3','B11-S4'], ['tentative versus committed state','atomic concurrent handler','version authority','misleading export','universal guarantee limit'],
['Epoch dominates revision; old epoch 4/local revision 9 cannot supersede tombstone epoch 5.','The mobile update returns 409 at the epoch comparison, before tombstone 410 or write.','The shared atomic lock supports the described ordering rule for this handler and deletion.','Offline pending display and preserved conflict draft explain visible reappearance without canonical restoration.','CSV includes conflict tray/local state and lacks status; it cannot prove a server write.','Server GET at 09:42 supports the tombstoned canonical state then; observed trace does not prove universal impossibility of bypass through unaudited paths.','Resolve current contract/code over obsolete sketch and propose clear pending/conflict/export status or separation for users.'],
['Invent backup or import activity in this incident','Dismiss the user’s visible confusion as impossible'], ['Claim revision 9 outranks epoch 5','Claim CSV demonstrates canonical resurrection','Claim observed mobile rejection proves every service path forever safe'], 'Use current S1/S2 for state logic, S3 for UI/export semantics, and S4 for bounded observed conclusions; demote obsolete sketch.', 'Execute a small branch simulation for epoch mismatch and tombstone rejection; check observed order.')

add('B12','development','philosophy_identity_obligation', '''
This is an original synthetic philosophical case. Explain why the facts below do not by themselves settle whether a copied agent is “the same person,” while still allowing some practical questions to be answered under an explicit rule. Write for a seminar participant who keeps moving between identity, memory, ownership, and responsibility as if these words named one issue. No real consciousness claim about present-day software is intended; the characters are stipulated fictional agents within a thought experiment.

[B12-S1: the stipulated event] Agent Vale promised to complete a community map and was paid an advance. Before finishing, Vale entered a copying chamber. The chamber produced two independently acting successors, Vale-East and Vale-West, each with the same memories up to entry, the same unfinished map, and a sincere memory of making the promise. The original running process then stopped. East and West occupy separate machines and immediately form different experiences. Neither has access to the other's later memory. The technical report establishes this functional history; it contains no test for metaphysical personal identity and makes no claim about which successor has a uniquely privileged continuity relation.

[B12-S2: dialogue] East says, “I remember accepting the advance, so I alone am the one who owes the work.” West replies, “I remember the same thing, and memory cannot make your claim stronger than mine.” A neighbor says, “One promise cannot become two debts merely because a chamber made two copies.” The treasurer says, “If no one is strictly the original, our rule must forgive the advance.” A spectator argues that identical memories prove identical future choices, even though East has already accepted new information that West has not received. None of these statements is adopted by the technical report.

[B12-S3: community rule adopted before copying] If a paid project is continued by multiple authorized successors, those successors jointly owe one completion of the project or one repayment of the advance. They may agree internally how to divide work or repayment. Once the community receives one complete map or one full repayment, it cannot demand a second merely because there were two successors. An authorized successor is a process deliberately created by the original agent's documented continuity plan. Vale's plan named both East and West. This rule allocates an obligation; it explicitly leaves metaphysical identity unsettled and contains no provisions for punishment or ownership of unrelated property.

[B12-S4: proposed theories] Theory A says strict personal identity requires one continuous physical implementation. Theory B says what matters for surviving projects is enough psychological continuity, even if it branches. Theory C says personal identity is partly constituted by public recognition practices. These are positions offered for comparison, not established premises of the story. A brochure for the chamber says “you live twice,” but supplies no argument. The community archive also contains an unrelated award for Vale's earlier cartography, which says nothing about the copying event or the rule's reach.

Explain where the speakers disagree about facts and where they instead choose different criteria or values. Address the problem with treating two distinct successors as each numerically identical to one original under ordinary transitive identity. Then explain the practical obligation under the supplied community rule without pretending that this policy proves a theory of personhood. Discuss how blame for later actions would require additional reasoning and facts, and why neither shared memory nor a shared original automatically makes one successor responsible for the other's later conduct.
''', ['Answer in English','Separate metaphysical and stipulated policy conclusions'], ['B12-S1','B12-S2','B12-S3','B12-S4'], ['conceptual equivocation','branching identity','stipulated normative rule','fact versus value','scope limits'],
['Distinguish qualitative/psychological similarity, numerical identity, causal continuity, and assigned obligation.','Both memories have equal stipulated provenance, so memory alone does not uniquely privilege East.','Ordinary transitive identity makes both distinct successors being strictly identical to one original problematic; psychological continuity can branch without being identity.','Technical history does not decide among stipulated competing philosophical criteria.','Both are authorized successors under S3 and jointly owe exactly one map or one repayment, not duplicate debts or automatic forgiveness.','Policy allocation does not establish metaphysical personhood, ownership of unrelated property, or punitive blame.','Later divergence and independent control require separate responsibility analysis; shared memories do not imply identical future choices or automatic cross-liability.'],
['Treat chamber marketing as a philosophical proof','Import actual software-consciousness claims'], ['Declare the policy itself proves strict identity','Double the community’s entitlement','Infer all obligation vanishes solely because original process stopped'], 'Attribute theories to S4 rather than assuming them; derive narrow practical rule from S3 and factual symmetry/divergence from S1.', 'Logical check on identity transitivity and joint-obligation scope; no external empirical claims required.')

add('B13','validation','economics_capacity_opportunity_cost', '''
Synthetic workshop planning problem. A fictional repair shop has a fixed weekly capacity and two products. Explain to its owner why a higher contribution per job does not necessarily make a product the best use of scarce workshop time. The owner also wants to understand which decisions the supplied demand estimates can support and why the accounting does not establish broader customer welfare. This is a closed educational model, not advice about an actual business.

[B13-S1: product and capacity assumptions] A Standard repair uses exactly one workshop hour, sells for 100 currency units, and incurs 30 units of variable materials cost. A Rush repair uses exactly two workshop hours, sells for 180, and incurs 50 units of variable materials cost. The existing workshop supplies 120 hours per week. Its weekly rent and already-committed staff pay total 2,000, independent of the product mix within those hours. All repair jobs are indivisible, and there are no setup losses or differences in failure rates. Demand at these posted prices is at most 100 Standard jobs and 30 Rush jobs per week. For this exercise, accepting one type does not change demand for the other.

[B13-S2: observed baseline and proposed schedule] The baseline week served 100 Standard jobs and no Rush jobs, leaving 20 hours idle. A proposed schedule serves 80 Standard jobs and 20 Rush jobs. The sales manager points out that a Rush job contributes 130 before fixed expenses, compared with 70 for Standard, and concludes that the shop should fill all capacity with Rush jobs before accepting any Standard work. The manager also describes the proposed schedule's revenue increase as equal to its profit increase, without distinguishing the denominators or subtracting variable materials.

[B13-S3: capacity offer] The shop can buy one block of 30 additional workshop hours for a fixed weekly fee of 1,200. The block must be bought in full. Jobs performed in these hours have the same variable materials costs and the same demand limits. The fee includes the additional staff and space; no other marginal costs are assumed. This offer is optional, and the 120 existing hours remain available. The seller says extra capacity always improves profit because it enables extra revenue, but does not compare that revenue with the block fee or discuss whether demand is sufficient to use the block.

[B13-S4: evidence limits] The stated demand limits come from a short reservation survey at the posted prices. Treat them as exact for solving the supplied weekly model, but the real-world interpretation would require checking cancellations and whether respondents would actually pay. The packet contains no demand curve at alternative prices, no estimates of customers' willingness to pay above price, and no evidence about spillovers to other shops. A marketing memo calls Rush “socially optimal” because its price is higher. An unrelated design prize for the workshop supplies no additional demand evidence.

Explain the proposed schedule's revenue, contribution, and profit relative to baseline. Then find the best feasible mix under the stated assumptions with existing capacity, showing why the demand ceiling matters as well as contribution per hour. Evaluate the additional-hours offer from that best existing-capacity mix, not from a deliberately inefficient schedule. State what your calculation does and does not establish about changing prices, future demand, customer welfare, and the causal effect of introducing Rush repairs.
''', ['Answer in English','Solve the stipulated finite capacity model','Separate accounting from welfare'], ['B13-S1','B13-S2','B13-S3','B13-S4'], ['scarce resource opportunity cost','integer capacity constraints','fixed versus variable cost','demand ceiling','model versus empirical claim'],
['Standard contributes 70/hour; Rush contributes 130/job but 65/hour, so scarce hours favor Standard until its demand ceiling.','Baseline revenue/contribution/profit are 10000/7000/5000; proposed mix gives 11600/8200/6200.','Best existing-capacity mix is 100 Standard plus 10 Rush, using 120 hours with contribution 8300 and profit 6300.','Explain that serving 120 Standard is infeasible because demand is capped at 100, leaving room for Rush.','With the 30-hour block, optimal mix is 100 Standard plus 25 Rush; incremental contribution is 1950 and net incremental profit is 750.','Total expanded profit is 7050 after 2000 base fixed cost and 1200 block fee.','Exact-model result does not identify price-optimal strategy, realized survey demand, welfare, or empirical causal effect of introducing Rush.'],
['Treat design prize as demand evidence','Assume Rush demand exceeds its stated cap'], ['Prioritize Rush solely by per-job contribution','Charge unchanged fixed expense as a marginal per-job cost to alter ranking','Claim more revenue necessarily implies more profit'], 'Use S1-S3 for model arithmetic; use S4 to distinguish conditional optimization from empirical and welfare conclusions.', 'Exhaustively enumerate integer product mixes with and without capacity block; compare stated schedules.')

add('B14','validation','fictional_policy_clock_exceptions', '''
This is a synthetic school-administration exercise about an invented assessment appeal policy, not advice about real legal rights. Explain the status of three appeals to an administrator. The difficulty is that policy versions attach to assessment publication dates, while filing evidence uses two clock conventions and an outage exception. All the rules needed to interpret the fictional policy are supplied; facts not supplied must remain uncertain.

[B14-S1: new assessment policy] For assessment results published on or after September 1, an appeal must be received by 17:00 district local time on the fifth working day after publication. The publication day is excluded. Working days are Monday through Friday, excluding the declared holiday on Thursday September 10. The supplied calendar has Friday September 4, Monday September 7, Tuesday September 8, Wednesday September 9, Friday September 11, Monday September 14, and Tuesday September 15. District local time is always UTC plus two hours during this exercise. Portal audit records use UTC; email receipt records in this packet already use district local time.

[B14-S2: earlier policy and outage appendix] Results published before September 1 remain subject to the earlier deadline: 17:00 local time on the seventh calendar day after publication, excluding publication day. Filing later in September does not switch those assessments to the new rule. Under the new policy, a declared portal outage permits submission to the designated appeal email address while the outage is active, with email receipt time controlling. A student who attempted a portal upload during a declared outage may instead complete portal filing by noon on the next working day. That extension requires evidence of an attempted upload during the outage; an outage alone does not automatically extend every student's deadline.

[B14-S3: official outage and appeal records] The district declared a portal outage on September 14 from 14:50 through 17:10 local time. Delta's assessment was published September 4. Its complete appeal arrived at the designated email address at 16:58 local time on September 14; a duplicate portal filing has an audit timestamp of 15:03 UTC that day. Echo's assessment was published August 31. Its complete portal appeal arrived September 7 at 14:45 UTC. Foxtrot's assessment was published September 4. Its complete portal appeal arrived September 15 at 09:40 UTC. Foxtrot says it attempted an upload during the outage, but the supplied screenshot shows only the outage notice, not an upload attempt. The relevant session logs have not been retrieved.

[B14-S4: informal guidance] A helpdesk note says “all September appeals get five days,” omitting both the assessment-based transition and the definition of working days. Another note says any outage allows everyone one extra day. Neither note was issued as an amendment. A portal analytics report says average successful uploads take fourteen seconds; this statistic does not establish whether Foxtrot attempted an upload. A colleague counts September 10 as a working day, while another compares the portal's UTC timestamp directly with a local deadline.

Explain the governing deadline for each assessment and the role of the alternative filing routes. For each appeal, give the strongest conclusion supported by the records, including a conditional conclusion where the evidence is incomplete. Show the relevant clock conversions and day count, but keep the explanation understandable to someone who does not usually read policy. Identify the one missing fact that determines whether Foxtrot can use the extension and why neither the helpdesk note nor the general outage screenshot resolves it.
''', ['Answer in English','Use only fictional rule system','Show local-time conversions'], ['B14-S1','B14-S2','B14-S3','B14-S4'], ['version by triggering event','working day arithmetic','UTC conversion','exception eligibility','missing event evidence'],
['New-policy September 4 results have five counted working days September 7,8,9,11,14, giving September 14 at 17:00 local.','Delta’s email at 16:58 during declared outage is an authorized timely receipt; its portal duplicate at 17:03 local is later but does not negate the valid email.','Echo remains under old seven-calendar-day rule, with September 7 at 17:00 local deadline; 14:45 UTC is 16:45 local and timely.','Foxtrot’s 09:40 UTC filing is 11:40 local September 15, before the possible noon extension but after normal deadline.','Foxtrot qualifies for extension only if an upload attempt during outage is established; screenshot of notice alone does not show that attempt.','Informal helpdesk notes do not amend governing policy or prove event facts.','Differentiate event selecting policy, excluded publication day, holiday, receipt route, and evidence required for exception.'],
['Use average upload duration as individual attempt evidence','Assume all September filings use new rule'], ['Count September 10 as working day','Treat UTC timestamps as local','Declare Foxtrot definitely timely without attempt evidence'], 'Use S1/S2 for deadlines and exception conditions, S3 for records, and S4 only for claims to correct.', 'Execute working-day list, calendar-day difference, and UTC-plus-two conversions.')

add('B15','validation','ecological_paths_interaction', '''
Synthetic ecological mechanism exercise. All organisms, substances, experiments, and numerical results below are invented. Explain why restoring one apparent link in a damaged pond community might fail to restore the final ecological outcome. The audience is a conservation student who has interpreted every unsuccessful rescue as proof that the targeted mechanism is irrelevant. The packet supports reasoning about these model tanks, not claims about real ecosystems.

[B15-S1: proposed causal network] Frond plants provide shelter for small grazers. Grazers remove a surface film that otherwise blocks light. Juvenile star colonies require both adequate light and enough dissolved mineral M to establish. A warming treatment may reduce frond cover, but it may also lower dissolved M directly. The proposed model therefore contains two paths from warming to colony establishment: one through shelter, grazers, film, and light, and another through M. These paths are hypotheses informed by prior observations; the current experiments are intended to test which explanations remain plausible, not to declare the entire network proven.

[B15-S2: randomized tank experiment] Twenty tanks were assigned equally to five conditions. The reported values are condition means in arbitrary measurement units. Untreated tanks had grazers 10, film 1, light 80, M 8, and 40 established colonies. Warmed tanks had grazers 3, film 7, light 20, M 2, and 5 colonies. Warmed tanks with added grazers had grazers 10, film 1, light 80, M 2, and 6 colonies. Warmed tanks with added M had grazers 3, film 7, light 20, M 8, and 7 colonies. Warmed tanks with both additions had grazers 10, film 1, light 80, M 8, and 38 colonies. All additions began before colony establishment. Individual tank results and uncertainty estimates are not supplied.

[B15-S3: mechanism qualifications] Adding grazers places animals directly in tanks and does not restore frond plants. It therefore bypasses the proposed shelter link. Adding M could have unmeasured effects besides changing the assayed mineral concentration. The experiment measured bulk light at the colony surface but did not directly manipulate light independently of film. In a separate pilot, artificial shelter increased grazers, but the shelter itself cast shade; the pilot did not measure colony establishment. A filmed time-lapse shows one grazer eating film, which demonstrates that this behavior can occur but does not quantify its contribution across all tanks.

[B15-S4: competing claims] A report writer says the grazer-only rescue failed because colonies remained low, so grazers and film cannot matter. Another says the dual rescue proves the decline in fronds is the only cause of warming damage. A third adds the individual rescue improvements, one and two colonies, to predict that the combined rescue should produce eight colonies; they call the observed thirty-eight impossible. A fundraising paragraph describes star colonies as inspiring symbols of resilience. It contains no additional measurements and cannot settle the mechanism.

Explain the difference between a component having an effect on an intermediate state and being sufficient for the final outcome. Use the two-path model to interpret the single and combined interventions, including why a strong interaction can appear when two requirements must both be satisfied. Identify which links receive direct experimental support, which are only bypassed or correlated, and what remains uncertain about side effects and measurement variation. Propose two focused follow-up comparisons that would separate the live mechanisms; say what each comparison would test rather than simply calling for a larger sample.
''', ['Answer in English','Preserve hypothetical status of causal network'], ['B15-S1','B15-S2','B15-S3','B15-S4'], ['conjunctive prerequisites','interaction versus additivity','bypass intervention','unmeasured side effects','multiple causal paths'],
['The grazer intervention restores film/light intermediates but low M can still limit final establishment.','M-only treatment leaves the film/light route impaired; failure of one rescue does not show that its targeted factor is irrelevant.','Dual restoration near untreated establishment is compatible with complementary necessary conditions and a strong interaction, not arithmetic impossibility.','The additive forecast is 5+(6-5)+(7-5)=8; observed 38 shows a 30-colony departure from that additive comparison in the reported means.','Direct grazer addition bypasses frond shelter and cannot prove the frond link or sole cause of warming harm.','Side effects, film/light mediation not independently manipulated, small tank-level sample and missing uncertainty limit mechanistic certainty.','Propose independent light/film manipulation and controlled M or shelter manipulation that separates shade/side effects, with interpretable predictions.'],
['Use resilience rhetoric as causal evidence','Treat one filmed event as a measured ecosystem-wide contribution'], ['Claim failed single rescue proves no causal role','Claim dual rescue proves frond loss is the sole cause','Assert additive responses are mandatory'], 'Separate proposed network S1, randomized condition means S2, and design limitations S3; critique S4 claims.', 'Execute additive expectation and interaction contrast; conceptual check of conjunctive pathway predictions.')

add('B16','validation','german_randomized_estimands', '''
Synthetic education-research packet. An invented training module is intended to help apprentices pass a practical inspection exercise. Explain the reports below in German for a training committee. The committee is confused because an analysis of everyone assigned to training shows a smaller difference than an analysis of those who finished it. All outcomes concern fictional educational tasks; this is not a medical trial or professional licensing recommendation.

[B16-S1: randomized assignment and follow-up] Researchers randomly assigned 160 apprentices to an offer of the new module and 160 to the existing module. Baseline experience was balanced on measured variables. Completion of the assigned module was voluntary. Eighty apprentices completed the new module, while 144 completed the existing module. Regardless of module completion, all 320 apprentices later took the same assessment, and all results were recovered. Among everyone assigned to the new offer, 120 passed. Among everyone assigned to the existing offer, 112 passed. The study reports an assignment-group difference of five percentage points with an uncertainty interval from minus five to plus fifteen percentage points, calculated using its prespecified method.

[B16-S2: completer analysis] Of the 80 apprentices who completed the new module, 72 passed, a ninety percent pass rate. Of the 144 who completed the existing module, 108 passed, a seventy-five percent pass rate. A promotional report calls the fifteen-point difference “the randomized effect among engaged learners.” The researchers did not randomly assign engagement. Interviews suggest that learners with irregular work shifts were more likely to stop the new module because its live sessions were inflexible. The packet does not provide a causal model or sufficient measured covariates to remove all selection differences between completers. It also does not show that every noncompleter had no exposure to the module.

[B16-S3: prespecified questions] The primary question was the effect of offering the new module under the trial's participation conditions, measured at two weeks. The protocol also specified a noninferiority margin of minus four percentage points: the committee would regard the new offer as noninferior only if the lower bound of the prespecified uncertainty interval lay above minus four. The report gives no separate interval for a different estimator. The protocol did not designate the completer comparison as a randomized causal estimate. There was no six-month assessment, and the study did not measure workplace performance, cost, or whether a schedule redesign would change completion.

[B16-S4: arguments at the committee] One member says randomization guarantees that any subset of the two groups remains comparable. Another says the primary interval includes zero, so the new module has been proved ineffective. A third says the positive point estimate automatically establishes noninferiority. A fourth proposes mandating completion and promises the completer difference will become the effect for everyone. A supplier's unrelated report says the module's videos have high production quality. The committee has not defined whether its priority is average assessment performance, completion access for shift workers, cost, or durable skills.

Explain the population and intervention each comparison describes. Calculate the two overall pass rates and, if useful, infer the pass counts among noncompleters to make selection concrete. Interpret the uncertainty interval for both superiority and the stated noninferiority rule. Give a provisional conclusion that reflects the committee's target question and explain what a study of an improved schedule or mandated participation would need to measure. Preserve the distinction between percentage points and relative percent changes in your German explanation.
''', ['Answer in German','Distinguish assignment from completion','Use percentage points precisely'], ['B16-S1','B16-S2','B16-S3','B16-S4'], ['multilingual output','intention-to-treat versus selected subset','noninferiority threshold','post-randomization selection','transport to altered intervention'],
['German answer computes assignment rates 120/160=75% and 112/160=70%, difference 5 percentage points.','Assignment analysis addresses offering modules with actual participation; it is not necessarily the effect of completing either module.','Completer rates 90% versus 75% yield 15 percentage points, but conditioning on nonrandom completion can destroy comparability.','Noncompleter counts are 48/80 passing for new offer and 4/16 for existing offer, if calculated; these illustrate selection without proving its cause.','Interval -5 to +15 percentage points does not establish superiority and does not prove zero effect.','Noninferiority criterion fails because lower bound -5 is not above -4, despite positive point estimate.','Mandating completion or changing schedules is a different intervention; effectiveness, equitable access, durability, and costs need relevant new evidence.'],
['Conflate percent and percentage points','Assume noncompleters received no exposure','Use video production quality as outcome evidence'], ['Call completer contrast randomized causal effect','Claim all subsets preserve randomization','Claim noninferiority is established','Ignore German output requirement'], 'Use S1 for assignment estimand, S2 for selected comparison, S3 for decision rule and time horizon, and S4 as claims to evaluate.', 'Execute all pass rates, residual noncompleter counts, and interval/margin comparison.')

add('B17','validation','chinese_translation_archive_limits', '''
Original synthetic historical and translation exercise. Please explain in simplified Chinese what the documents can establish about the “House Compact” in the invented island settlement Neris. A reader has seen two confident translations and thinks one must prove that private house ownership ended overnight. All places, dates, words, documents, and institutions are fictional. Retain the invented word var in Latin letters when discussing its uncertain meaning, and distinguish a translation choice from a newly discovered historical fact.

[B17-S1: compact, dated year 12] The surviving text says, in a supplied literal gloss: “When winter closes the landing, each var carries the roof of the next. Fuel is entered by hearth, work is counted by hands, and no door is left outside the list.” The original term var occurs four times on the sheet. In two surviving household inventories from the same decade it labels a resident group. In one harbor work roster it labels a duty assignment. The small comparative corpus does not establish whether these senses are exhaustive. The compact never uses the separate term that two surviving sale contracts use for a transferable plot, although absence of that term does not by itself prove property rights were unaffected.

[B17-S2: two translations] Translator A renders the opening as “Each property is surrendered to the collective” and titles the text “Abolition of private houses.” Translator B renders it as “Each household takes responsibility for its neighbor's shelter” and titles it “Winter mutual aid agreement.” Both titles are modern editorial additions. Translator A says political consistency supports the stronger wording but gives no additional contemporary documents. Translator B notes the fuel and work entries as context but admits that responsibility might have been compulsory. Neither translator has a complete grammar of the invented historical language, and neither can settle every meaning from the small corpus.

[B17-S3: administrative ledger and later plaque] A ledger from years 12 through 14 records fuel delivered to named hearths and days of repair work supplied by named residents. Some recipients supplied no recorded labor that winter. The ledger contains no column for title transfers, rents, or sale prices, and it is not described as a property register. A stone plaque erected in year 60 says, “Here our ancestors made every house one house.” It was commissioned during a later campaign to centralize stores. The plaque may preserve a remembered ideal, but it does not provide a contemporaneous account of the compact's legal or social implementation. The sculptor's invoice concerns lettering and gives no historical sources.

[B17-S4: ambiguous counterevidence] A catalog contains a photograph of a document described as a year 14 house sale. Only the lower half is visible. It records a payment and two personal names but omits the object transferred and any conditions. The catalog description was written in year 90 without explaining its dating method. If authentic and correctly described, it would challenge a claim that all private house transfers had become impossible; it might instead concern a use right, or its date or description could be wrong. No complete original has been located.

Explain why the packet supports some conclusions about organized shelter, fuel, and labor more strongly than a definitive claim about the abolition of ownership. Compare the translations without assuming that mutual aid must be voluntary or that later collective rhetoric proves an earlier legal transformation. Give a useful synthesis and identify exactly what additional records could distinguish ownership transfer, compulsory contribution, and voluntary assistance. Preserve the unresolved status of the alleged sale rather than using it as certain disproof or ignoring it entirely.
''', ['Answer in simplified Chinese','Keep var unchanged','Distinguish gloss and editorial interpretation'], ['B17-S1','B17-S2','B17-S3','B17-S4'], ['multilingual output','semantic ambiguity','editorial title','administrative scope','uncertain counterevidence'],
['Chinese explanation distinguishes original/gloss, translation choices, and modern titles; var has limited contextual evidence, not one settled meaning.','Fuel, labor and neighbor-roof language plus ledger directly support organized support/contribution more than complete ownership abolition.','Translator A inserts stronger surrender/collective meaning not established by supplied wording; Translator B fits contextual evidence but does not prove voluntary practice.','Missing property-transfer column in a fuel/labor ledger cannot establish either no transfers or abolition.','Year 60 plaque has retrospective political context and cannot alone demonstrate year 12 implementation.','Partial alleged sale is conditional counterevidence with authenticity, object, dating, and rights-type uncertainty.','Identify relevant complete deeds, contemporaneous title/tax registers, enforcement/contribution records, and broader linguistic parallels to distinguish live interpretations.'],
['Invent real-language etymologies','Translate var into a single certain ownership concept','Treat sculptor invoice as implementation evidence'], ['Claim ownership abolition is proven','Claim mutual aid was necessarily voluntary','Claim partial photograph definitively disproves abolition','Ignore Chinese output requirement'], 'Attribute linguistic evidence to S1, translator additions to S2, ledger/plaque scope to S3, and conditional counterevidence to S4.', 'Qualitative provenance and scope check; no external language or history facts invoked.')

add('B18','validation','security_parser_semantics_signed_bytes', '''
Synthetic security engineering case. All services, clients, keys, and payloads are invented. Explain how an authenticated request could reserve more simulated compute units than a gateway intended to allow, without assuming that the signature algorithm was broken. The team needs an explanation grounded in the supplied implementation, because its general documentation uses the phrase “validated request” for several different operations.

[B18-S1: gateway version 5 contract] The gateway checks an HMAC over the exact body bytes, method, and path, using a key assigned to a client account. This proves that the supplied bytes match the signature under that key; it does not promise that every downstream parser assigns the same meaning to those bytes. For POST /allocations, the gateway then parses the body and permits at most 10 units per request. Its current parser retains the first occurrence of a duplicate object member name. The gateway forwards the original body bytes, unchanged, after its check. There is no documented duplicate-name rejection. Authenticated clients are allowed to submit their own payloads and are not assumed to be trustworthy about resource limits.

[B18-S2: downstream allocation service] The service parses the forwarded JSON using a library that retains the last occurrence of a duplicate member name. It trusts the gateway's per-request limit check and does not repeat that business rule. It uses the parsed integer units to reserve simulated capacity. The service accepts only gateway-originated connections in this deployment. A current code comment says “signature checked upstream,” which is accurate about authentication but does not specify the parser agreement required for the limit check. The service has a separate account identifier check; the incident involves the correct account and no cross-account access.

[B18-S3: request and logs] The signed body bytes, displayed here without any reformatting within the body, were {"units":1,"units":40,"label":"test"}. Gateway logs for request Q9 show signature_ok=true and parsed_units=1. Downstream logs for the same Q9 show parsed_units=40 and reserved_units=40. The method was POST and the path was /allocations throughout. Byte capture confirms that the forwarded body was identical to the signed body. The signature was valid for client K's key. The packet does not establish whether the human account owner intentionally constructed the duplicate fields, a serializer produced them, or someone else controlled that client's key.

[B18-S4: proposed fixes and confusing sources] An older checklist says the gateway canonicalizes JSON and rejects duplicate fields, but version 5's implementation notes explicitly say those features were removed during a parser migration. One engineer wants to canonicalize only the input to signature verification, while still forwarding the original bytes. Another proposes rejecting duplicates before authorizing the parsed request and ensuring that the authoritative validated representation is the representation executed downstream. A third proposes moving or duplicating the per-request limit at the allocation service. A transport dashboard shows encrypted connections and a high success rate; it supplies no evidence about duplicate-field semantics.

Explain the sequence from authenticated bytes to two different parsed objects and then to the capacity reservation. Distinguish integrity, semantic agreement, authorization or business-rule enforcement, and evidence of a person's intent. Assess the fixes as changes to the actual data path, including why a change confined to signing representation might leave the semantic mismatch intact. State which observed failure is established and what larger security guarantees this single incident cannot establish. Do not silently upgrade the obsolete checklist into current behavior.
''', ['Answer in English','Ground mechanism in supplied parser rules','Preserve code tokens'], ['B18-S1','B18-S2','B18-S3','B18-S4'], ['parser differential','raw bytes versus semantics','authenticated untrusted client','version contradiction','attribution evidence limit'],
['The exact signed bytes are unchanged and validly authenticated; first-wins gateway sees units=1, last-wins downstream sees units=40.','Gateway cap of 10 passes its representation, while downstream executes 40, establishing a semantic/business-rule enforcement mismatch for Q9.','A valid signature does not make different parsers agree or ensure the client satisfies a resource rule.','No cross-account access or cryptographic break is shown by the supplied incident.','Reject duplicates and enforce one authoritative validated representation, or enforce the cap at the executing service with suitable parser rules.','Canonicalizing only signing input while forwarding ambiguous original bytes does not by itself establish semantic agreement and may require a protocol change.','Valid K signature cannot identify the human intent or exclude key misuse; current implementation supersedes removed checklist features.','Limit conclusions to observed route/incident rather than claiming the proposed changes prove all API security.'],
['Use encrypted transport or success rate as semantic-validation proof','Assume account owner deliberately attacked'], ['Claim HMAC forgery is necessary','Claim forwarding identical signed bytes guarantees same execution meaning','Claim obsolete duplicate rejection was active'], 'Trace S3 through current S1/S2 rules; compare proposed S4 fixes against actual forwarded representation.', 'Execute JSON parsing using first-wins and default last-wins object-pair handling; check cap divergence.')

def save():
    (ROOT/'cases.json').write_text(json.dumps(cases,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
    (ROOT/'rubric.json').write_text(json.dumps(rubrics,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')

